-- *Admission rule.* Owner, verbatim: «среды которые проверенно не умеют выключать тулы (а также для которых нет обходных путей отключить тулы с гарантией) мы пока не поддерживаем». Environments that provably cannot disable their tools, and have no guaranteed workaround, are not supported for now. The Muse row of R3 is therefore not admitted for donors: its read tools cannot be removed and the Seatbelt read boundary is not yet accepted as a guarantee; it stays a lab row. Pi and OpenCode keep `tool_posture_unsupported`: the source research TASK-261010-aqpf2a (curator board) found candidate configurations for Pi 1.1.0 and OpenCode 1.18.35 that remove their built-in tools from the model's tool set, and each must be qualified under R7 before a row exists.
-- *Strict qualification at launch.* Owner, verbatim: «для удаленного работника нужна будет более строгая политика запуска сессии так что бы могли запускать только проверенные без оптимистичного поддержания новых». A remote-worker session starts only an exact qualified tuple of R3. The executor checks the executable's digest against the qualified allowlist **before exec**; an unknown, newer or unqualified build refuses `tool_posture_unsupported`, with no warning-only path; a self-updating launcher, or a link to one, is never executed; a last-known-good fallback may select only another qualified tuple. The allowlist comes from the central qualification feed (the known-good rows of agents-management), never from a host-local list. For Muse this is the `strict-qualified` version-policy profile designed in skill-project-management TASK-261010-1j7osl (amendment 3 of TASK-261009-33sey0); Curator selects that profile for every remote-worker session, and that work moves up in its queue when this launcher needs it.
